Keep it thorough but readable. Let me write.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-14377a1f591040a899ff353664ffcbdb", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-14377a1f591040a899ff353664ffcbdb", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "# Experiment Design — Does Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting Improve Task-Solving Ability of LLM-Based Agents in WorkArena++?\n\n**Status: DESIGN ONLY. No experiment has been run; no results are claimed.** This document is a decision-complete plan intended to be executed elsewhere. All benchmark-specific details are tagged **[ASSUMPTION]** where they come from domain knowledge and could not be verified in this workspace (which contains only the project brief `task.json`, no repositories, no datasets, no network, and no relevant installed packages).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research question and hypotheses\n\n- **RQ:** Does adding a chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting mechanism — an explicit prompt directive that makes the LLM produce intermediate reasoning before each web action — improve the task-solving ability of an LLM-based agent on WorkArena++?\n- **Primary hypothesis (H1):** Mean task success rate under the CoT-prompted condition is higher than under the no-CoT baseline, on a matched set of WorkArena++ task instances.\n- **Secondary hypotheses:** (H2) Any observed advantage persists when the CoT arm is compared against a length-matched \"verbosity\" control that adds the same quota of extra text without task-relevant reasoning — i.e., the effect is attributable to reasoning *content*, not merely to additional tokens or instruction salience. (H3, exploratory) The effect of CoT varies by task family and difficulty (e.g., larger gains on multi-step knowledge/order-management tasks than on single-step form-fill).\n- **Operational definitions:** *CoT* = model-generated intermediate reasoning text, elicited by a prompt directive placed in a fixed position in the context before each action, distinct from the action itself. *Task-solving ability* = environment-oracle per-task binary success; aggregated as mean success rate over matched instances.\n\n## 2. Benchmark setup — WorkArena++\n\n- **Environment [ASSUMPTION]:** Official WorkArena++ suite built on **BrowserGym**, driving a ServiceNow-backed application UI via **Playwright**. WorkArena++ extends the WorkArena benchmark (ICLR 2024) with additional/harder task families; it is run through `browsergym`-style environments exposing tasks as (template, parameter set) pairs. Family taxonomy is assumed ~20–30 families (e.g., Knowledge, Service Catalog, Order Management, Software, Sales, ...); exact count/taxonomy to be confirmed from the pinned repo at execution time and recorded verbatim.\n- **Agent loop [ASSUMPTION]:** Standard BrowserGym agent scaffold. At each step the model receives (i) the task instruction, (ii) a serialized AOM accessibility tree of the current page, (iii) the action history (truncated window), and emits an action in the agent action space (`click`, `fill`, `select_option`, `type`/`press_enter`, `hover`, `scroll`, `submit`, `goto`, `close`, `go_back`, etc.). **A reasoning/`think` field is assumed available in the action schema (see §3/§9)**; if not present, the design falls back to free-form CoT text in the assistant message before a JSON action line (fallback documented below).\n- **Scoring [ASSUMPTION]:** Binary per-episode success from the environment's automated success checker (oracle) that verifies the ServiceNow state against the goal; for answer/instruction-following families, success is exact-match on the produced answer, judged by the official checker. No human evaluation in the primary loop.\n- **Episodes/caps [ASSUMPTION]:** Max 20 environment steps per episode; 60 s per step; applied identically to all arms. Episodes exceeding the cap count as failures.\n- **Environment provisioning [ASSUMPTION]:** Self-hosted ServiceNow instance via the official dockerized setup, pinned to a fixed version/commit; instance state reset to a clean baseline before every episode; the repo commit SHA, instance version, and any observed UI drift (DOM hash + screenshot) logged per episode for reproducibility.\n\n## 3. Conditions and agent architecture\n\nThree arms (same scaffold, same observation format, same action schema, same model/checkpoint; **the only textual difference across arms is the reasoning-directive block in the system prompt, in a fixed position**):\n\n| Arm | System-prompt directive (diff line) | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| **A. Baseline (no-CoT)** | *\"Choose the single best next action and output its JSON. Do not write any commentary or explanation.\"* If the scaffold has a reasoning field, it is suppressed/forbidden. | Reference |\n| **B. CoT (free-form)** | *\"Before choosing the action, think step by step: restate the goal, assess what has been done, identify what remains, and justify the best next action; write this reasoning in the reasoning field, then output the action JSON.\"* | Treatment |\n| **C. Length-matched verbosity control** | *\"Before outputting the action, write exactly N tokens of commentary that describes the visible page layout in plain words (no planning, no goal analysis), then output the action JSON.\"* N matched to arm B's realized median reasoning length in tokens. | Confound guard |\n\n- **Pairing/counterbalancing:** Arms share all other prompt text verbatim, prompt position of the directive block, task ordering, and instance ordering. To limit order/learning effects, arm order per instance is randomized (balanced) across instances; a fresh session/episode is used per arm per instance (each episode is independent, so no within-episode contamination).\n- **Fallback (if scaffold exposes no reasoning field):** Arm B emits CoT as ordinary assistant text preceding a JSON action line; Arm C emits the length-matched filler text similar